1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,569
Why does an AI Agent repeat your best work instead of building on it?

2
00:00:03,519 --> 00:00:05,644
The answer is not a longer prompt.

3
00:00:05,644 --> 00:00:09,201
It is a reusable skill that carries the parts of the workflow worth repeating.

4
00:00:09,201 --> 00:00:13,759
OpenAI describes skills as reusable, shareable workflows for specific tasks.

5
00:00:13,759 --> 00:00:20,500
A skill can carry a name, instructions, and supporting resources so the next run starts from a useful operating pattern rather than a blank chat.

6
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:29,663
The loss is familiar: you finally get a good research brief, handoff, analysis, or draft, then reconstruct the same context, constraints, and formatting rules next week.

7
00:00:29,663 --> 00:00:33,028
The result is inconsistency before the real work even begins.

8
00:00:33,028 --> 00:00:42,057
In this video, you will build an AI Agent Skill Template with seven fields: trigger, inputs, workflow, output contract, guardrails, evidence, and revision note.

9
00:00:42,057 --> 00:00:45,153
It is small enough to use and explicit enough to inspect.

10
00:00:45,153 --> 00:00:49,307
Before we begin, save the idea that a useful skill does not replace judgment.

11
00:00:49,307 --> 00:00:53,826
It removes the avoidable setup work so your judgment can focus on the parts that are genuinely new.

12
00:00:53,826 --> 00:00:56,807
Look for a repeat that has a recognizable starting signal.

13
00:00:56,807 --> 00:01:01,403
For example, a weekly customer summary starts when fresh notes and product data arrive.

14
00:01:01,403 --> 00:01:05,259
The skill should describe that trigger, not merely say that it creates summaries.

15
00:01:05,259 --> 00:01:09,355
A repeatable task has stable intent, even when the source material changes.

16
00:01:09,355 --> 00:01:15,769
If the goal, audience, and success condition are different every time, you still have exploration, not a workflow ready to package.

17
00:01:15,769 --> 00:01:18,557
Do not mistake frequent copying for repeatability.

18
00:01:18,557 --> 00:01:26,230
A copied prompt may contain a useful sentence, but a skill earns its place when the same decisions, checks, and deliverable shape recur across several real runs.

19
00:01:26,230 --> 00:01:31,596
Write the trigger in ordinary language: when a decision-ready update is needed from new evidence, use this skill.

20
00:01:31,596 --> 00:01:35,932
That sentence prevents a good workflow from being activated for a nearby but different job.

21
00:01:35,932 --> 00:01:38,663
Next, list the inputs that change the answer.

22
00:01:38,663 --> 00:01:44,413
A source folder, a customer brief, a target audience, a deadline, and an approved vocabulary can matter.

23
00:01:44,413 --> 00:01:46,884
Old decorative examples usually do not.

24
00:01:46,884 --> 00:01:50,192
Resources work best when they sit beside the step that needs them.

25
00:01:50,192 --> 00:01:56,221
A research workflow can point to the source standard during evidence collection and to the delivery template only when the final brief is assembled.

26
00:01:56,221 --> 00:01:58,067
Make constraints concrete.

27
00:01:58,067 --> 00:02:06,355
Instead of saying be careful, specify what must not be invented, which records are authoritative, which action requires approval, and how uncertainty should be shown to the reviewer.

28
00:02:06,355 --> 00:02:08,875
This is also where a skill differs from memory.

29
00:02:08,875 --> 00:02:11,673
Memory can remember a preference or prior fact.

30
00:02:11,673 --> 00:02:16,759
A skill tells the Agent which context to retrieve, how to use it, and what to do when it conflicts.

31
00:02:16,759 --> 00:02:19,817
Now write the workflow as a short sequence of decisions.

32
00:02:19,817 --> 00:02:26,923
Begin with the goal, inspect the inputs, identify missing information, choose a method, create the draft, and verify the promised outcome.

33
00:02:26,923 --> 00:02:29,269
Each step needs a reason to exist.

34
00:02:29,269 --> 00:02:33,096
If a step only repeats what the previous step already required, remove it.

35
00:02:33,096 --> 00:02:37,644
If a step changes the evidence, the choice, or the handoff, make that change visible.

36
00:02:37,644 --> 00:02:39,442
Use branches sparingly.

37
00:02:39,442 --> 00:02:45,355
A useful branch sounds like this: if the source is incomplete, ask for the missing record and label the draft as provisional.

38
00:02:45,355 --> 00:02:48,269
It does not try to predict every imaginable exception.

39
00:02:48,269 --> 00:02:51,269
For high-risk claims, demand an evidence move.

40
00:02:51,269 --> 00:02:56,423
The Agent can cite the record, show a calculation, name an assumption, or ask a human to decide.

41
00:02:56,423 --> 00:02:59,509
A confident sentence alone is not a verification step.

42
00:02:59,509 --> 00:03:02,134
A good skill ends with a handoff contract.

43
00:03:02,134 --> 00:03:06,971
Define the audience, format, required fields, and the decision the output should support.

44
00:03:06,971 --> 00:03:10,894
This lets a reviewer check the result without reverse-engineering the original prompt.

45
00:03:10,894 --> 00:03:12,855
Include an uncertainty rule.

46
00:03:12,855 --> 00:03:21,115
If a source is missing, if two records disagree, or if a decision depends on human preference, the output should surface that condition instead of burying it in fluent language.

47
00:03:21,220 --> 00:03:23,477
The final check should match the task.

48
00:03:23,427 --> 00:03:26,158
A release note needs factual and link checks.

49
00:03:26,158 --> 00:03:29,120
A customer message needs audience and permission checks.

50
00:03:29,120 --> 00:03:32,889
A plan needs owners, next steps, and unresolved decisions.

51
00:03:32,889 --> 00:03:34,764
This makes skills shareable.

52
00:03:34,764 --> 00:03:38,802
Another teammate does not need your private intuition to judge whether the run succeeded.

53
00:03:38,802 --> 00:03:43,245
They can inspect the input, the decisions, the output contract, and the evidence.

54
00:03:43,245 --> 00:03:45,975
Do not freeze a skill after its first success.

55
00:03:45,975 --> 00:03:54,158
Record the failure pattern that led to a revision: a missing input, a misleading default, a weak output field, or a check that looked reassuring but missed the real error.

56
00:03:54,158 --> 00:03:55,754
Keep the unit small.

57
00:03:55,754 --> 00:04:01,446
One skill can gather evidence, another can turn evidence into a decision brief, and a third can run a release check.

58
00:04:01,446 --> 00:04:05,293
Smaller skills are easier to understand, test, and recombine.

59
00:04:05,293 --> 00:04:07,841
Retest after a revision on fresh work.

60
00:04:07,841 --> 00:04:12,168
Replaying the exact example that inspired the skill proves only that you preserved the past.

61
00:04:12,168 --> 00:04:15,927
A new input reveals whether the instructions carry the right judgment forward.

62
00:04:15,927 --> 00:04:22,975
When a skill becomes too long, ask which part is truly shared and which part belongs in a resource, a template, or a separate specialized skill.

63
00:04:22,975 --> 00:04:26,610
The goal is reliable guidance, not a monument to every past lesson.

64
00:04:26,610 --> 00:04:28,735
The practical sequence is simple.

65
00:04:28,735 --> 00:04:37,350
Capture a real repeat, name the trigger, bring in the context that changes decisions, write the few steps that matter, define the handoff, and test the result against a fresh case.

66
00:04:37,350 --> 00:04:42,283
That is how an AI Agent stops replaying your best prompt and starts using a maintained working method.

67
00:04:42,283 --> 00:04:44,802
The skill does not make the Agent infallible.

68
00:04:44,802 --> 00:04:48,437
It makes the work easier to inspect, improve, and reuse.

69
00:04:48,437 --> 00:04:51,446
Try the template on one small recurring task this week.

70
00:04:51,446 --> 00:05:00,504
Write the trigger in one sentence, attach only the source material that changes a decision, name one check a reviewer can actually perform, and record one uncertainty that the Agent must not smooth over.

71
00:05:00,504 --> 00:05:03,254
If that version helps a second real run, keep it.

72
00:05:03,254 --> 00:05:07,937
If it fails, revise the field that failed instead of adding unrelated instructions everywhere.

73
00:05:07,937 --> 00:05:09,668
Follow Tiny Agent.

74
00:05:09,668 --> 00:05:12,264
Tiny Agent helps you get better at using AI.
